vCalendar Authentication - the GET user/hash credential pair is deprecated


1. SUMMARY

   The vCalendar endpoint (vcalendar.php) used to accept a credential in the
   query string:

       http://host/vcalendar.php?user=clerk&hash=<md5 of the password>

   That path is DEPRECATED and is now DISABLED BY DEFAULT. A request that
   supplies `user` and `hash` and no HTTP Basic credential is answered with
   401 Unauthorized. Nothing else about the endpoint changes: an authenticated
   client still gets the same text/x-vCalendar body.

   The reason is that the value it compared was not a one-time token. It was
   compared directly against the stored `user.userpassword` value - an unsalted
   MD5 digest of the password - so anyone who could read that digest (a
   database read, a backup, a log, a SQL injection elsewhere) could present it
   as the `hash` parameter and be authenticated without ever knowing the
   password. This is pass-the-hash: the digest IS the credential. A value that
   is being compared to a stored secret cannot also serve as the secret itself.

   The digest is still compared with MD5 for Basic authentication, because
   moving the password storage format is a separate change. What changed is
   that the digest is no longer accepted AS the credential, and the comparison
   is done in PHP with hash_equals() against the row fetched by username
   rather than in SQL as `userpassword = MD5('<pass>')`.


2. WHAT A CLIENT AUTHOR MUST DO

   Clients must migrate to HTTP Basic authentication over TLS:

       https://host/vcalendar.php               (no query credential)
       Authorization: Basic <base64(user:password)>

   Notes for client implementers:

     * TLS is required, not optional. Basic sends the password itself in a
       header on every request. Over plain HTTP that is strictly worse than
       what is being removed, so do not migrate to Basic over http://.
     * A plain `curl -u user:password https://host/vcalendar.php` works. The
       endpoint issues the 401 challenge with
           WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm="<package> v<version> vCalendar"
       and clients that wait for the challenge (rather than pre-emptively
       sending the header) will be prompted normally. This was broken on
       PHP 8 - the challenge died inside the endpoint before it was sent - and
       is repaired as part of the same change.
     * The rest of the endpoint's interface is unchanged:
       `?physician=`, `?type=fromdate`, `?m=`, `?d=`, `?y=` still work, and are
       still what a client sends to select a calendar. They are now validated
       as integers, and an impossible date (for example m=13) is refused with
       400 Bad Request instead of being silently normalised by mktime().
     * `?physician=<id>` still names a provider, but it is now enforced against
       the authenticated identity: a caller may read its own provider's
       calendar, and any other provider's calendar only with the
       `schedule`/`view` access control grant (see section 4). A caller
       without it gets 403 Forbidden. Admin account user id 1 is permitted
       through freemed::acl()'s own admin short-circuit.


3. THE TEMPORARY COMPATIBILITY SWITCH

   A site that cannot migrate its calendar clients immediately may turn the
   deprecated path back on for the duration of the migration. It is an option
   in the `config` table, so it is set in the FreeMED UI (Administration ->
   Configuration) or directly:

       INSERT INTO config (c_option, c_value, c_title, c_section, c_type)
       VALUES ('vcals_legacy_hash', 'off', 'vCalendar legacy GET hash auth',
               'Scheduler', 'YesNo');

   and then switching `c_value` to a true value ('1', 'on', 'yes' or 'true').

   DEFAULT: OFF. With no `vcals_legacy_hash` row at all - which is the state of
   every install that has not deliberately created one - the GET user/hash path
   does nothing and returns 401.

   Every use of the compatibility path is logged at LOG_NOTICE (syslog priority
   133 on this stack; 134, which appears on the LOG_INFO lines in the same
   logs, is LOG_INFO),
   whether or not the credential is accepted:

       vCalendar [get] DEPRECATED hash authentication used (vcals_legacy_hash=on),
       claimed username = <user>, remote = <ip>

   The username is logged as CLAIMED and deliberately before verification: for
   a pass-the-hash attempt that is the only audit trail there is. Search the
   syslog for "DEPRECATED hash authentication" to find the clients that still
   need migrating, and turn the option off again when the log is quiet.

   This endpoint writes the deprecation notice to syslog at LOG_NOTICE because
   that is the channel the migration instructions grep; the project's own
   LOG__SECURITY constant (lib/macros.php:119) records its other auth events on
   the SystemLog sink, which no operator here is watching. The switch cannot be
   turned on by anything in a request - only by editing the config table.


4. OPERATOR PREREQUISITE - THE schedule/view ACL

   READ THIS BEFORE UPGRADING IF ANYONE BUT AN ADMINISTRATOR READS
   PROVIDERS' CALENDARS.

   When `?physician=` names a provider other than the caller's own, the
   endpoint now requires the same grant the scheduler UI requires:

       freemed::acl('schedule', 'view')

   On a schema where that grant has never been created, the check fails and the
   request is refused with 403. That is the fail-closed direction, and it is
   deliberate: an authorization object that cannot be evaluated must never
   widen access. But it also means the upgrade can REMOVE A CAPABILITY THAT
   PREVIOUSLY WORKED for a legitimate non-physician scheduler (a front desk or
   practice-manager account that used to fetch providers' calendars).

   So, as part of the upgrade, a site that needs that capability must grant it:
   create the `schedule` / `view` ACO and the ARO for the roles that need it
   (in the FreeMED ACL administration, the same `schedule`/`view` pair the
   scheduler module itself uses), and verify with the affected role. Non-admin
   accounts that only ever read their OWN provider's calendar need no grant;
   they are unaffected. The administrator account (user id 1) needs no grant
   either - freemed::acl() short-circuits to true for it.

   Also note: the ACL object is loaded on demand by this endpoint because a
   request that defines SESSION_DISABLE - as this one does - skips the block in
   lib/freemed.php that normally builds it. If the vendored phpGACL cache
   directory under /tmp is not writable by the web user, the check cannot be
   evaluated and the request is refused (403), not permitted. Make sure the web
   user owns its cache directory, as on a normal install.


5. RELEASE NOTE

   Release note - vCalendar authentication

   The vCalendar endpoint no longer accepts a password digest in the query
   string (`?user=<name>&hash=<md5 of the password>`). It was a
   pass-the-hash vulnerability: the stored digest of the password was compared
   directly against the value the client supplied, so anyone who could read the
   digest could authenticate with it without knowing the password.

   Clients must migrate to HTTP Basic authentication over TLS. A site that
   cannot migrate immediately can set the `vcals_legacy_hash` option in the
   `config` table to a true value to restore the old path for the duration of
   the migration; it is OFF by default and every use is logged. Operators
   should also grant the `schedule`/`view` ACO to any non-administrator role
   that needs to read a provider's calendar other than its own, because the
   provider scoping added by this release requires that grant and otherwise
   returns 403.


6. SEE ALSO

